From a downstream security review, 2026-11-04: a GPU manager writes Skeid's node list into a ConfigMap (nodes.yaml) that Skeid reads by mtime reload. Today Skeid reads exactly one config file, and a node entry may name any api_key_env and any api_key_ref. Whoever can write that list (a compromised manager) could make Skeid send any environment variable or broker secret as a node key to a URL of its choosing.
Want: the main config names extra node files with constraints, e.g.
  node_files:
    - path: /etc/skeid/extra/nodes.yaml
      allow_api_key_env: '^SKEID_NODE_EXTRA_[A-Z0-9_]+_API_KEY$'
      allow_api_key_ref: false
      allow_url_hosts: [ '*.node-manager.svc' ]
      allow_kinds: [ documents, chat ]
A node from such a file that breaks a constraint is rejected (reload status names file, position and rule, never the value); the rest of the file still loads; the file only contributes nodes (no policies, keys, aliases). Reload by mtime per file, all-or-nothing per file. Interplay with the admin API and customer_source/node_source as for file nodes today.
Alternative to weigh in the ADR: a deployment's own Skeid class (ADR 0030, k111) reading the list itself — but the constraint is generic, every deployment with a second writer needs it.
Order: after k111 (one writer per repo). Until Skeid can, a deployment may merge the two files in a sidecar with this allowlist.

